  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186133827,
      "id": "da23f1c0-678a-51c0-a2db-e12d756f9120",
      "title": "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ade24ec4-18c1-5b20-ba13-ec725ddad092",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y + 1}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2},t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le t\\le \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(t - 1)\\,\\mathbf{j}$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=t - 1$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 1)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(4 t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(1)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y + 1}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1(2 t^{2})}{(t - 1)-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t^2}{t} \\\\  & =2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}^{\\sqrt{3}}(2 t)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=16 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(32 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{9}^{49}\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{9}^{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[(49)^{3/2}-(9)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[(343)-(27)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{343}{24}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
